(.nif27)
; Vendor map for SEG-Y files whose textual header says that the source and receiver line and point
; numbers are times 100 in the trace headers ("SOURCE AND RECEIVER LINE AND POINT NUMBERS ARE X 100 IN
; TRACE HEADERS"). SOURCE: the textual header and trace headers of two such files: the four numbers are
; 32-bit integers in bytes 181-196, which Rev 0 leaves unassigned. The order (receiver line, receiver point,
; source line, source point) was checked against the files' survey (SPS) stations; the divisor is the
; text's "X 100". A point number with a fraction (100.5) is stored as 10050; the keys hold the quotient,
; exact for a whole number.
; The OFFSET (bytes 37-40) is stored in the same units as the coordinates, though the sentence does not
; say so, so it is read through the coordinate scalar (bytes 71-72), as the coordinates are: in every
; trace with an offset of one shot record of such a file, the stored offset is 100 times the distance
; between the source and receiver coordinates of the same trace, and the coordinates carry the
; scalar -100. This was seen on one file only. A trace with an unset offset (0) stays unset: the keys then
; derive it from the coordinates.
; RECOGNITION: a SEG-Y file whose textual header holds the words below, so a file without the sentence is
; read as before. The map reads no other field than these and has no target to decode.
(vendormap "segy-x100-stations" (version 1)
  (match (format segy) (textContains "LINE AND POINT NUMBERS ARE X 100 IN TRACE HEADERS"))
  (doc "SEG-Y trace headers that hold source and receiver line and point and the offset times 100")
  (layout
    (entry rec_line (at 181) (type int4) (divisor 100))
    (entry rec_point (at 185) (type int4) (divisor 100))
    (entry sht_line (at 189) (type int4) (divisor 100))
    (entry sht_point (at 193) (type int4) (divisor 100))
    (entry offset (at 37) (type coor4))))
